{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfs41y8aWFidCGZ64gQo7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I dismissed R4F1 as out of scope. The verdict is committed as `929ddc66` in `docs/reviews/wi-834/005-ADJUDICATE-7a8e2d5.md`, with the `WI: WI-834` trailer.\n\n**The bypass is real.** I checked all three of the reviewer's forms and the builder's fourth one, `echo \"$\\⏎(…)\"`, at 705f8784:\n- The lexer, `shell_line.segments`, reads each one as plain data (`[['cat']]`, or one `echo` word). The hook decides from those words, so it allows them inside an armed window.\n- In Git Bash, with `echo LAUNCHED` standing in for the model command, all four actually run the command.\n\n**Why it's out of scope anyway:**\n- **Normal commands are caught.** I tried the continuations an agent actually writes: across an argument list, after a `timeout` or `env` prefix, after a pipe or `&&`, inside `$( )`, and inside a here-document body before a substitution. The lexer reads every one and the hook denies it.\n- **Only deliberate token-splitting gets through.** The gap opens only when a continuation breaks up something the reader looks for: the `$(` opener, or a here-document's end marker. No ordinary command does that. Its only use is to sneak a launch past the hook, which is the \"not malice\" case PROCESS §6 excludes.\n- **The spec doesn't promise to stop a determined evader.** It calls the hook \"supervision within that coverage, not inspection of arbitrary shell programs\". `bash -c '…'` and `eval` are allowed by design, and ruling (f) makes the coordinator's stop encouraged, not enforced. An evading agent already has easier ways out.\n- **It isn't a regression of shipped behaviour.** The fault is in the lexer this lane added.\n\nFollowing §6, I recorded the dismissal in one line and asked for no code. If the coordinator wants to keep this from coming back in a later round, the place is the hook's coverage statement at the